@RestController → Marks class as REST endpoint.

@RequestMapping("/hello") → Base path for all APIs inside this controller.

@GetMapping("/{name}") → GET requests are used to fetch or read data (like loading a web page or getting info).

@PostMapping -> POST requests are used to send or create new data to the server (e.g., submitting a form).

@PathVariable -> Path segment (part of the URL itself)

@RequestParam -> Query parameter (after ? in URL)

@RequestBody -> used to bind the JSON (or XML) in the HTTP request body to a Java object

@ResponseEntity - return both body and HTTP status code together, e.g.ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND).body(errorObject);

@ConfigurationProperties -> Automatically fill this Java class with values from my config file

@Value -> Inject values from properties/environment into Java fields

@Query ->Used for Querying(SELECT Query)

@Transactional -> Used for Modifying operation in DB,also for maintains atomicity in Spring DB

@Modifying → Marks query as update/delete

@Modifying(clearAutomatically = true) --> Automatically clears cached entities, ensuring fresh data on next query.

@Valid - checks if the incoming request data follows the rules you defined in your Java class (like not null, valid email, etc.)

Constraints annotation:
| Annotation                   | Applies To                         | Meaning                                      |
| ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| `@NotNull`                   | Any type                           | Field cannot be `null`                       |
| `@NotEmpty`                  | Strings, collections               | Must not be `null` **and** must not be empty |
| `@NotBlank`                  | Strings                            | Must not be `null` or whitespace             |
| `@Size(min, max)`            | Strings, collections               | Must be between given length                 |
| `@Min(value)`                | Numbers                            | Must be greater than or equal to value       |
| `@Max(value)`                | Numbers                            | Must be less than or equal to value          |
| `@Positive`                  | Numbers                            | Must be > 0                                  |
| `@PositiveOrZero`            | Numbers                            | Must be ≥ 0                                  |
| `@Negative`                  | Numbers                            | Must be < 0                                  |
| `@DecimalMin("value")`       | BigDecimal, double                 | Must be ≥ value                              |
| `@DecimalMax("value")`       | BigDecimal, double                 | Must be ≤ value                              |
| `@Email`                     | String                             | Must be a valid email format                 |
| `@Pattern(regexp = "...")`   | String                             | Must match given regex                       |
| `@Past`                      | Date, LocalDate                    | Must be a past date                          |
| `@Future`                    | Date, LocalDate                    | Must be a future date                        |
| `@AssertTrue`                | Boolean                            | Must be true                                 |
| `@AssertFalse`               | Boolean                            | Must be false                                |
| `@Digits(integer, fraction)` | Numbers                            | Limit integer/fraction digits                |
| `@Length(min, max)`          | Strings (from Hibernate Validator) | Specifies character count range              |

@Validated -> Spring-specific annotation (from org.springframework.validation.annotation.Validated)
that is used to trigger validation on:
method parameters
method return values
or bean properties

| Use Case                                                                | Recommended Annotation            | Why                                  |
| ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| You have one implementation now, but plan more later                    | `@Primary`                        | Makes one bean default automatically |
| You need to switch beans dynamically (like “contract” vs “permanent”)   | `@Qualifier`                      | Gives you explicit control           |
| You have multiple beans and different classes should use different ones | Combine `@Primary` + `@Qualifier` | Best of both                         |
